  "rule_description": "Java SE 11 removes the java.corba module (JEP 320). The org.omg.CORBA, org.omg.CosNaming, org.omg.PortableServer and related packages no longer exist in the JDK runtime image, so any source referencing org.omg.* fails to compile on JDK 11. JEP 320 states there will be no standalone CORBA unless third parties take over maintenance, and that RMI-IIOP has no standalone version at all. In JADE the whole CORBA surface is one component: the idlj-generated IDL stub package FIPA (42 files generated from src/fipa.idl) plus the single IIOP message transport jade/mtp/iiop/MessageTransportProtocol.java, which is the only file in the workspace that imports package FIPA. The knowledge graph shows zero incoming edges into that component from the other 972 workspace files, and the default MTP selected by jade/core/ProfileImpl.java is jade.mtp.http.MessageTransportProtocol. The dependency is therefore deleted, not re-supplied from an external jar. Because the fix is deletion of a whole component, the rule flags two constructs: (1) a source reference to a removed org.omg.* type, and (2) membership of the generated FIPA stub package itself, which exists only to carry those references. Five of the 42 generated files carry no org.omg reference of their own (AgentID, DateTime, Envelope, FipaMessage, ReceivedObject) yet cannot survive the deletion of their siblings: FipaMessage declares a FIPA.Envelope[] field and ReceivedObject a FIPA.DateTime field. Construct (2) is what makes the component complete in the flag index, and therefore complete in the shard plan.",
  "rule_id": "CORBA_REMOVAL",
  "rule_name": "Remove the CORBA dependency (org.omg.*) instead of retrofitting it",
